WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 8: Share Your Views.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.740
Welcome to Unit 8!

3
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.480
In this unit you will learn how people
share their views and try to persuade others.

4
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.960
Persuasive writing and speaking are everywhere:
in posters that ask you to join an event,

5
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.380
in letters to the headteacher asking for something
new, in film reviews that recommend a movie,

6
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.660
and even in the conversations you
have with your friends every day.

7
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.080
By the end of this unit, you will know how to
tell the difference between a fact and an opinion,

8
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.380
how to use powerful language to persuade an audience,

9
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.400
and how to structure a piece of writing
that makes people sit up and listen.

10
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.640
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:50.360
By the end of this unit, you will be able to recognise and
write persuasive texts such as posters, letters, and reviews.

12
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.940
you will also tell the difference between a fact (something
that can be proved) and an opinion (what someone thinks or

13
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.400
feels).

14
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.880
you will also use comparative and superlative
adjectives to make your arguments stronger.

15
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.340
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

16
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:16.000
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: intelligence,
citizenship, physician, geographer, ancient, magnificent.

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.480
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

18
00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.040
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

19
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:32.050
The reading sequence includes Share Your Views:
source text 1 and Share Your Views: source text 2.

20
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.340
Read actively.

21
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:38.200
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

22
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.180
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

23
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.060
The first language focus is The Present Perfect Tense.

24
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:54.100
What it means and why it matters The present perfect tense
is a special way to talk about actions that happened in the

25
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.540
past but are still important now.

26
00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.120
It connects the past with the present.

27
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.160
We use it to talk about experiences,
achievements, and situations that continue.

28
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:10.960
For example, "She has studied philosophy and
physics" means she studied those subjects before,

29
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.740
and that knowledge is still relevant today.

30
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:20.540
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

31
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:27.140
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Present Your Views.

32
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:32.400
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

33
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.260
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

34
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.360
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

35
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.220
You are ready to begin.

36
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:48.900
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

37
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.540
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

38
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.320
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
